# Sentence embeddings

An embedding model maps a piece of text to a fixed-length list of numbers (384 or 768 floats for the small models we looked at). The training objective pushes paraphrases towards the same point and unrelated text apart, so **geometry stands in for meaning**: "my laptop won't turn on" and "the notebook computer doesn't boot" end up neighbours even though they share almost no words.

## How the small models are trained

- start from a BERT-style encoder;
- pool the token outputs (mean pooling, or the [CLS] token);
- fine-tune with a contrastive loss on (query, relevant passage) pairs with in-batch negatives: the correct pair should score higher than every other passage in the batch.

## Measuring closeness

Cosine similarity, or plain dot product when the vectors are normalised to length 1. Retrieval = embed the query, return the passages with the highest scores.

## Asymmetric retrieval

Queries are short, passages are long. Several models (bge, leaf) want a prefix on the **query only**, e.g. "Represent this sentence for searching relevant passages: ".

## Matryoshka (MRL) training

Some models are trained so the first 256 or 384 dimensions already work on their own. You can cut the vector and re-normalise to save memory with a small quality loss.

## Limits

Numbers, negation and rare names are weak spots; a keyword method still wins for exact identifiers like `ERR_CONNECTION_RESET`. That's why hybrid search exists.
